# Performance Evaluation

## Findings

### PERF-001: Synchronous I/O Operations Block the Event Loop

| Field | Value |
|-------|-------|
| **Rule ID** | PERF-001 |
| **Severity** | High |
| **Category** | I/O Optimization / Concurrency Utilization |
| **Location** | Lines 6, 10-11 (`writeFileSync`, `readFileSync`, `existsSync`) |

**Description:**
All three filesystem operations — `writeFileSync` (line 6), `existsSync` (line 10), and `readFileSync` (line 10) — are synchronous. In any Node.js server context (which "sessions" strongly implies), these calls block the event loop for the entire duration of the disk I/O. During that time, no other requests can be processed. Session load/save is inherently a per-request hot path, so this directly impacts request throughput and tail latency for all concurrent users.

`loadSession` is particularly expensive: it performs two synchronous syscalls (`stat` via `existsSync`, then `open`/`read` via `readFileSync`) plus a `JSON.parse`, all blocking.

**Recommendation:**
Use async filesystem APIs and let the event loop continue serving other requests while I/O completes.

```typescript
import { promises as fs } from "fs";

async function saveSession(sessionId: string, data: any): Promise<void> {
  await fs.writeFile(`${SESSION_DIR}/${sessionId}.json`, JSON.stringify(data));
}

async function loadSession(sessionId: string): Promise<any> {
  const path = `${SESSION_DIR}/${sessionId}.json`;
  try {
    const content = await fs.readFile(path, "utf-8");
    return JSON.parse(content);
  } catch (err: any) {
    if (err.code === "ENOENT") return null;
    throw err;
  }
}
```

This also eliminates the TOCTOU race between `existsSync` and `readFileSync` (the file could be deleted between the two calls) by using a single `readFile` with error handling.

**Impact:** On a server handling concurrent requests, synchronous file I/O can stall the event loop for 1–10+ ms per call depending on disk. Under 100 concurrent session operations, this serializes into 100–1000+ ms of cumulative blocking, degrading p99 latency for the entire application.

